<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What Thinking About Policing is — evidence-based writing, a live crime dashboard and a free research search — and the Sussex Police Inspector behind it."
>
  <article>
    <header class="border-b border-ink-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-10 py-14 md:py-20">
        <p class="font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent mb-5">
          About
        </p>
        <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl font-semibold text-ink-900 leading-[1.05] tracking-tight">
          About this site, and the person writing it.
        </h1>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-10 py-12 md:py-16">
      <div class="prose-article">
        <h2>What this site is</h2>

        <p>
          Thinking About Policing is evidence-based writing on policing and public policy, aimed at senior police leaders, policymakers, academics, and frontline officers who want something with more thought behind it than the usual commentary. The pieces start from data or research where it exists, acknowledge limits where the evidence is contested, and try to land a point rather than just survey the field.
        </p>

        <p>
          But it's no longer just writing. The site now has two working tools alongside the articles:
        </p>

        <p>
          The <a href="/data">Crime Dashboard</a> pulls live street-level data from data.police.uk and the Home Office's recorded-crime series, so you can explore what's actually happening in a force area — and ask questions of the numbers — without wrestling a spreadsheet.
        </p>

        <p>
          The <a href="/research">Research Assistant</a> is a free search of the open scholarly record. Most policing research sits behind paywalls a working officer will never cross; ask it a question in plain English and it searches 250 million works, sketches what the evidence says, and links straight to the free, legal copy where one exists. No library card needed.
        </p>

        <h2>What's here to read</h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Police Policy</strong> covers the operational and organisational side of the job — misconduct prevention, officer wellbeing, use of force, neighbourhood policing, evidence-based practice. <strong>Public Policy</strong> is wider: criminal justice, social policy, the political context that shapes what police are asked to do. <strong>Influential People</strong> profiles the thinkers whose work changed how I see policing. <strong>Data Stories</strong> lets the numbers do the arguing. And <strong>Interesting Distractions</strong> is for whatever the restless brain wanders into — the Somerton Man code, an appreciation of <em>More or Less</em>, and the rest.
        </p>

        <h2>The person writing it</h2>

        <img
          src="/images/nathan-tracey.jpg"
          alt="Nathan Tracey"
          loading="lazy"
          class="w-full max-w-sm rounded-xl shadow-sm mx-auto my-10"
        />

        <p>
          I'm Nathan Tracey, a Police Inspector with Sussex Police, based in Hampshire. I came to the job the long way round — years managing a Tesco department, joining at thirty-one, diagnosed with ADHD at thirty-seven. That diagnosis, honestly, saved my life: a lot of what I write traces back to the gap between how people are assumed to function and how they really do.
        </p>

        <p>
          I work in neighbourhood policing and spend a fair amount of time on data — officer assaults, use of force, misconduct trends — with a bias towards early intervention. None of it came from a statistics degree. It started with Tim Harford's <a href="/other/more-or-less-and-the-art-of-not-being-fooled-by-numbers/">More or Less</a>, grew through <a href="/influential-people/jerry-ratcliffe-and-the-case-for-intelligence-led-policing/">Jerry Ratcliffe's</a> work on intelligence-led policing, and owes a great deal to <a href="/influential-people/alex-murray-and-the-rise-of-evidence-based-policing/">Alex Murray</a>, founder of the Society of Evidence-Based Policing. The conviction underneath all of it: an officer's instinct is a hypothesis, not a verdict — and checking it is the interesting part. (The <a href="/other/cracking-the-somerton-man-code/">Somerton Man's coded note</a> is the long-running puzzle on the side.)
        </p>

        <h2>A note on views</h2>

        <p>
          Everything here is written in a personal capacity. The views are mine and don't represent Sussex Police, any other employer, or any organisation I'm associated with. Where I draw on research, I'll cite it. Where I'm offering a personal read, I'll say so.
        </p>

        <h2>Get in touch</h2>

        <p>
          The comments on each article don't require a login, so please join the conversation — questions, challenges, and different perspectives are all welcome. If you'd rather email, you can reach me at <a href="mailto:ntracey@gmail.com">ntracey@gmail.com</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
